    ('7c6a563b-df2f-4c13-b743-76efc1a67382'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A Maya city whose temple-pyramids rise 71 meters above the plaza, with over 100 carved monuments. It waged war against Palenque and captured one of its kings.', 'At Tonina in Chiapas, a skeletal stucco death god carries the severed head of a lord of Palenque. The city also holds the last known Long Count date on any Maya monument, AD 909.', 'teaser-card-s014:7c6a563b-df2f-4c13-b743-76efc1a67382:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 178 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '30cfd89712e221a10a34270f8c32b60acb0181aad922a2975cf971c664757743|83e808c4cd33f109497a939e191ac53362ce0fced02efa914791e17ae1390be9|83e808c4cd33f109497a939e191ac53362ce0fced02efa914791e17ae1390be9', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-014", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At Tonina in Chiapas, a skeletal stucco death god carries the severed head of a lord of Palenque. The city also holds the last known Long Count date on any Maya monument, AD 909."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-014 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is Tonina, in Chiapas [S1]; a stucco sculpture at the site depicts a skeletal death god [S5]; the death god carries the severed head of a lord of Palenque [S5]; the city holds the last known Long Count date on any Maya monument [S4]; that date is AD 909 [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-041 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Tonina is a Maya archaeological site and ruined city (a settlement/city) in the Mexican state of Chiapas [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tonin%C3%A1]; At Tonina a stucco sculpture depicts a skeletal death god [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tonin%C3%A1]; The death god carries the severed head of a lord of Palenque [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tonin%C3%A1]; The city holds the last known Long Count date on any Maya monument [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tonin%C3%A1]; That last known Long Count date is AD 909 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tonin%C3%A1]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('7ceced42-5339-4dc0-84da-5c11f6898879'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A passage grave from around 4200 BC whose interior stones are covered in densely carved spirals, arcs, and concentric patterns. The most elaborately decorated megalithic tomb in Europe.', 'On Gavrinis, a small island in the Gulf of Morbihan, a 14 m-long passage leads to a Neolithic chamber. Of the 29 slabs lining it, 23 are carved with symbols and patterns.', 'teaser-card-s014:7ceced42-5339-4dc0-84da-5c11f6898879:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 170 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '19295fcc18a93a5bae9d329505bb03fc8741547369aa1ad67aeeff4bd1f95142|431bcbc2f9736b166d6edc0e03d9010b831f9eb2d2c6ad1f7e24cb770322d282|431bcbc2f9736b166d6edc0e03d9010b831f9eb2d2c6ad1f7e24cb770322d282', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-014", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On Gavrinis, a small island in the Gulf of Morbihan, a 14 m-long passage leads to a Neolithic chamber. Of the 29 slabs lining it, 23 are carved with symbols and patterns."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-014 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Gavrinis is a small island [S1]; the island lies in the Gulf of Morbihan [S1]; a passage 14 m long leads to a chamber [S6]; the chamber is Neolithic (part of the Neolithic passage grave) [S2, S6]; 29 slabs line the passage [S7]; 23 of the slabs are carved with symbols and patterns [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-041 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Gavrinis is a small island in the Gulf of Morbihan (Brittany, France) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gavrinis]; The island holds a passage tomb (the site is a passage tomb under a cairn) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gavrinis]; The chamber is Neolithic [SUPPORTED: https://cairndegavrinis.com/en/discover/]; A passage leads to the chamber [SUPPORTED: https://cairndegavrinis.com/en/discover/]; The passage is 14 m long [SUPPORTED: https://cairndegavrinis.com/en/discover/]; The passage is lined by 29 slabs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gavrinis]; 23 of the 29 slabs are carved with symbols and patterns [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gavrinis]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('7cff9dcb-732c-45c9-82be-8ee3096abcaa'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The site consists of at least 15 houses on an area of over 600 m2, with undercutting and subsequent demolitions that have continuously reduced the area.', 'Aghios Epiktitos Vrysi, at least 15 houses on a cliff 9 km east of Kyrenia, left awls and needles of a developed textile craft, and just one piece of obsidian, then extremely rare in Cyprus.', 'teaser-card-s014:7cff9dcb-732c-45c9-82be-8ee3096abcaa:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 190 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '4481dedc1ac6b1f03e2559c4c5c267acc4ac59d74ecafd3a9fd530311f0b5c34|9726e913954fa7b7470f49d0b17e139e98952912ca1f33228cf2f102ad0ebd70|9726e913954fa7b7470f49d0b17e139e98952912ca1f33228cf2f102ad0ebd70', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-014", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Aghios Epiktitos Vrysi, at least 15 houses on a cliff 9 km east of Kyrenia, left awls and needles of a developed textile craft, and just one piece of obsidian, then extremely rare in Cyprus."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-014 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is Aghios Epiktitos Vrysi [S1]; the site has at least 15 houses [S2]; the site is on a cliff [S1]; the site is 9 km east of Kyrenia [S1]; awls and needles were left there [S3]; the awls and needles testify to a developed textile craft (processing) [S3]; only one piece of obsidian was found there [S5]; obsidian was then extremely rare in Cyprus [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-041 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Aghios Epiktitos Vrysi is an archaeological site in Cyprus [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aghios_Epiktitos_Vrysi]; It lies on a cliff 9 km east of Kyrenia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aghios_Epiktitos_Vrysi]; The site is a settlement (a village) of houses [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aghios_Epiktitos_Vrysi]; The site consists of at least 15 houses [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aghios_Epiktitos_Vrysi]; It left awls and needles [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aghios_Epiktitos_Vrysi]; The awls and needles show a developed textile craft [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aghios_Epiktitos_Vrysi]; Just one piece of obsidian was found at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aghios_Epiktitos_Vrysi]; Obsidian was then extremely rare in Cyprus [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aghios_Epiktitos_Vrysi]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('7d24037c-4afc-49e7-a13e-713769bf8208'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The royal dynasty at Zapote Bobal seems to have flourished for only 200 years, disintegrating by A.D. 800.', 'On a leveled hill in the Petén, Zapote Bobal raised a royal palace and temple-pyramids, yet its royal dynasty seems to have flourished for only 200 years, disintegrating by A.D. 800.', 'teaser-card-s014:7d24037c-4afc-49e7-a13e-713769bf8208:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 182 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '84237dba64e437a6b747be234139810915e3099364a44eccc65042c242be67c8|2948656ea7a6bb3a87ba8009cd2054baeb4463cc2bb38b2b947b9d8aa9cf9a11|2948656ea7a6bb3a87ba8009cd2054baeb4463cc2bb38b2b947b9d8aa9cf9a11', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-014", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a leveled hill in the Petén, Zapote Bobal raised a royal palace and temple-pyramids, yet its royal dynasty seems to have flourished for only 200 years, disintegrating by A.D. 800."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-014 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is Zapote Bobal, in the Petén [S1]; the site is built on a leveled hill [S3]; it has a royal palace [S3]; it has temple-pyramids [S3]; its royal dynasty seems to have flourished for only 200 years [S6]; the dynasty was disintegrating by A.D. 800 [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-041 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Zapote Bobal is a pre-Columbian Maya archaeological site (a city with a royal center) in the Petén department of Guatemala [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zapote_Bobal]; The site was built on a leveled hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zapote_Bobal]; The center of the site has a royal palace [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zapote_Bobal]; The center of the site has temple-pyramids [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zapote_Bobal]; Its royal dynasty seems to have flourished for only 200 years [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zapote_Bobal]; The royal dynasty was disintegrating by A.D. 800 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zapote_Bobal]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('7d24f980-7f63-4e6a-8bb1-1f83d16435f2'::uuid, 'card_description', '8th millennium BC Early Neolithic mound in Thessaly, excavated by Marija Gimbutas. Practically untouched, it is packed wall-to-wall with Neolithic debris and hundreds of earthen mounds.', 'Achilleion in Thessaly is a tell of 200 by 260 m, layered from the Early and Middle Neolithic. Digs led by Marija Gimbutas uncovered pottery, bone tools, personal ornaments and stone tools.', 'teaser-card-s014:7d24f980-7f63-4e6a-8bb1-1f83d16435f2:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 189 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '9a6f3abd7ff1530aaebdfe37c506332eb593233d58099fe6cd99c1c42834f944|cac32200651d0dd1b3e23f25da13e5f395f0b4d7b6c3dc2a5a3637acaedf9232|cac32200651d0dd1b3e23f25da13e5f395f0b4d7b6c3dc2a5a3637acaedf9232', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-014", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Achilleion in Thessaly is a tell of 200 by 260 m, layered from the Early and Middle Neolithic. Digs led by Marija Gimbutas uncovered pottery, bone tools, personal ornaments and stone tools."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-014 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Achilleion is in Thessaly [S1]; it is a tell of 200 by 260 m [S3]; it is layered (stratified) from the Early and Middle Neolithic [S3]; the excavations were led by Marija Gimbutas [S4]; the excavations uncovered pottery [S5]; the excavations uncovered bone tools [S5]; the excavations uncovered personal ornaments [S5]; the excavations uncovered stone tools [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-041 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Achilleion is a settlement site (a tell) in Thessaly, Greece (central claim: what kind of place and where) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Achilleion_(Thessaly)]; Achilleion is located in Thessaly, Greece [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Achilleion_(Thessaly)]; The site is a tell [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Achilleion_(Thessaly)]; The tell measures 200 by 260 m [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Achilleion_(Thessaly)]; The tell is layered (stratified) and has Early Neolithic layers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Achilleion_(Thessaly)]; The tell is layered (stratified) and has Middle Neolithic layers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Achilleion_(Thessaly)]; The excavations (digs) were led by Marija Gimbutas [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Achilleion_(Thessaly)]; The excavations uncovered pottery [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Achilleion_(Thessaly)]; The excavations uncovered bone tools [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Achilleion_(Thessaly)]; The excavations uncovered personal ornaments [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Achilleion_(Thessaly)]; The excavations uncovered stone tools [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Achilleion_(Thessaly)]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('7d7e62e6-943f-4a2d-b5fa-8afd589ccf80'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The site was identified and first excavated by António Inácio Marques da Costa in 1906.', 'Atop Louro Hill, a scarp guarding its south, the Castro of Chibanes was first occupied in about 2900 BCE. Its Copper Age people farmed, kept livestock and gathered molluscs from the sea.', 'teaser-card-s014:7d7e62e6-943f-4a2d-b5fa-8afd589ccf80:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 186 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '63db076c229945f6880843e27b55543f5fd823efd145cc4be685f1d843374cc2|82c6045b7f3256ff3dcc6d96b8148d66fd9e445b6b882b0a128107ba301ad97c|82c6045b7f3256ff3dcc6d96b8148d66fd9e445b6b882b0a128107ba301ad97c', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-014", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Atop Louro Hill, a scarp guarding its south, the Castro of Chibanes was first occupied in about 2900 BCE. Its Copper Age people farmed, kept livestock and gathered molluscs from the sea."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-014 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site lies atop Louro Hill [S1, S2]; a scarp guards (naturally defends) the south of the hill fort [S2]; the castro was first occupied in about 2900 BCE [S3]; its occupants in the Copper Age farmed (agriculture) [S3, S5, S6]; its occupants kept livestock [S6]; its occupants gathered molluscs from the sea [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-042 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Castro of Chibanes is a castro (hill fort), i.e. a fortified hilltop settlement, in Portugal [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castro_of_Chibanes]; It lies atop Louro Hill (Serra do Louro), in the municipality of Palmela, Portugal [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castro_of_Chibanes]; A scarp guards the site on its south side [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castro_of_Chibanes]; The site was first occupied in about 2900 BCE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castro_of_Chibanes]; The first occupants belong to the Copper Age (Chalcolithic) [SUPPORTED: https://www.cm-palmela.pt/viver/museu-e-patrimonio/patrimonio-cultural/patrimonio-arqueologico/castro-de-chibanes]; Its people farmed and kept livestock [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castro_of_Chibanes]; Its people gathered molluscs from the sea [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castro_of_Chibanes]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('7d93ed41-cb1c-4f2c-a935-d81b4fa7d87c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A Megalithic temple from 2500 BC paired with a Bronze Age fortified village -- the earliest known fortification on the island. Cart ruts and silos surround the complex.', 'Over St George''s Bay, Borġ in-Nadur holds a megalithic temple turned into dwellings, a Bronze Age village with a D-shaped bastion, and Mycenaean sherds that hint at contact with the Aegean.', 'teaser-card-s014:7d93ed41-cb1c-4f2c-a935-d81b4fa7d87c:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 189 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '83bbbb157a041ce594046324e211023469cd7e8896881977fd0a54e0cf391b59|b29ca43b5d35407f27eea45441b9af2dc3736c2ed9e343e21b65325461e4ec30|b29ca43b5d35407f27eea45441b9af2dc3736c2ed9e343e21b65325461e4ec30', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-014", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Over St George''s Bay, Borġ in-Nadur holds a megalithic temple turned into dwellings, a Bronze Age village with a D-shaped bastion, and Mycenaean sherds that hint at contact with the Aegean."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-014 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site overlooks St George''s Bay [S1]; the site holds a megalithic temple [S2, S3]; the temple was converted into dwellings [S5]; the site holds a Bronze Age village [S2]; the village has a D-shaped bastion [S8]; Mycenaean sherds were found at the site [S6, S7]; the Mycenaean sherds indicate (hint at) contact with the Aegean [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-042 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Borġ in-Nadur is an archaeological site (a prehistoric temple and settlement complex) overlooking St George''s Bay, near Birżebbuġa, Malta [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bor%C4%A1_in-Nadur]; The site holds a megalithic temple [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bor%C4%A1_in-Nadur]; The megalithic temple was turned into dwellings in the Bronze Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bor%C4%A1_in-Nadur]; The structure was adapted for domestic use in the Bronze Age (second source for the temple-to-dwellings claim) [SUPPORTED: https://heritagemalta.mt/explore/borg-in-nadur/]; The site includes a Bronze Age village [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bor%C4%A1_in-Nadur]; The Bronze Age village had a D-shaped bastion [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bor%C4%A1_in-Nadur]; Sherds of Mycenaean origin were found at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bor%C4%A1_in-Nadur]; The Mycenaean sherds hint at contact between Malta and the Aegean [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bor%C4%A1_in-Nadur]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('7e40873d-033a-4bef-ba20-f4109219a1b3'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Muyil was one of the earliest and longest inhabited ancient Maya sites on the eastern coast of the Yucatan Peninsula.', 'On the Sian Ka''an lagoon, a name meaning Where the Sky is Born, lie the Maya ruins of Muyil, on a Caribbean trade route that once carried jade, obsidian, chocolate, honey and feathers.', 'teaser-card-s014:7e40873d-033a-4bef-ba20-f4109219a1b3:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 184 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '6a947e5dafd67b66bf690b6ff2eea52dab4551821d61a3a0ae6e9e9861159b1e|d986084b53e234688de44f357bc8bbef4002f388b42dcaf8f68e233b0e5ddc46|d986084b53e234688de44f357bc8bbef4002f388b42dcaf8f68e233b0e5ddc46', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-014", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the Sian Ka''an lagoon, a name meaning Where the Sky is Born, lie the Maya ruins of Muyil, on a Caribbean trade route that once carried jade, obsidian, chocolate, honey and feathers."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-014 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Muyil lies on the Sian Ka''an lagoon [S4]; the name Sian Ka''an means Where the Sky is Born [S4]; Muyil consists of Maya ruins [S1, S3]; Muyil lay along a trade route on the Caribbean [S5]; the trade route once carried jade, obsidian, chocolate, honey and feathers [S5, S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-042 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Muyil is a site of Maya ruins (an ancient Maya settlement) in Quintana Roo, Mexico [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muyil]; The Maya ruins of Muyil are in Mexico (Quintana Roo) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muyil]; Muyil is situated on the Sian Ka''an lagoon [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muyil]; The name Sian Ka''an means Where the Sky is Born [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muyil]; Muyil lay on a trade route on the Caribbean [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muyil]; The city became important as part of the coastal trade networks of the Yucatan peninsula (second source for the trade-route claim) [SUPPORTED: https://www.inah.gob.mx/zonas/zona-arqueologica-de-muyil]; The trade route carried jade, obsidian, chocolate, honey and feathers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muyil]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('7e97d2a5-3f87-44fc-9ad0-f0d5a2d84cab'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A rocky butte used by the Hohokam from around 1100 AD, with fifty bedrock mortars ground into exposed granite. A natural spring nearby drew early settlers.', 'On the western flank of the Santa Rita Mountains, a young girl exploring the crevices of Huerfano Butte found a small pottery jar filled with about 1,500 beads and pendants.', 'teaser-card-s014:7e97d2a5-3f87-44fc-9ad0-f0d5a2d84cab:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 173 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '71f229bb8ebd324c4fbc79f26001403b276a4b7f79cda5b64369b68ea2fcd62b|79fb705fb2ebbd895d107e328188d607ed4230aedeeea99939d4d0b99da8718a|79fb705fb2ebbd895d107e328188d607ed4230aedeeea99939d4d0b99da8718a', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-rewrite-v-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the western flank of the Santa Rita Mountains, a young girl exploring the crevices of Huerfano Butte found a small pottery jar filled with about 1,500 beads and pendants."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-v-002 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Huerfano Butte is on the western flank of the Santa Rita Mountains [S1]; a young girl explored the crevices of the butte [S7]; she found a small pottery jar [S7]; the jar was filled with about 1,500 beads and pendants [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-verify2-004 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Huerfano Butte is a small rocky butte and important prehistoric archaeological site on the western flank of the Santa Rita Mountains, Pima County, Arizona [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huerfano_Butte_(Arizona)]; A young girl exploring the cracks and crevices of the butte found the cache [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huerfano_Butte_(Arizona)]; The find was a small pottery jar [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huerfano_Butte_(Arizona)]; The jar was filled with about 1,500 beads and pendants [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huerfano_Butte_(Arizona)]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('7ff04b48-cfda-41a9-9ad0-8d84c58510f0'::uuid, 'card_description', 'One of the original seven gates of Roman London''s city wall, dating to the 3rd century AD. By the 12th century, parts of the gate had become a notorious prison.', 'Newgate, one of the six London Wall gates dating back to Roman times, sent its road west over the River Fleet. From the 12th century parts of its buildings held a gaol, later Newgate Prison.', 'teaser-card-s014:7ff04b48-cfda-41a9-9ad0-8d84c58510f0:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 190 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '7a99b43e9f221e986ab5c306eaecef66ba3c6a3049f91d9aec3b17b996d61e1f|0ffca32de7b8d64919f963c2f5a3375a144d0058fd9dff16caf5bc8d00168ace|0ffca32de7b8d64919f963c2f5a3375a144d0058fd9dff16caf5bc8d00168ace', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-014", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Newgate, one of the six London Wall gates dating back to Roman times, sent its road west over the River Fleet. From the 12th century parts of its buildings held a gaol, later Newgate Prison."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-014 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Newgate was one of the London Wall gates [S1]; it was one of the six gates dating back to Roman times [S1]; its road headed west over the River Fleet [S2]; from the 12th century parts of its buildings were used as a gaol [S3]; the gaol later developed into Newgate Prison [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-042 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Newgate was a gate of the London Wall (the wall around the City of London) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newgate]; Newgate stood on the site of a gate in the Roman London Wall (second source for the central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://www.londonmuseum.org.uk/collections/london-stories/history-londons-newgate-prison/]; Newgate was one of the six London Wall gates dating back to Roman times [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newgate]; The Roman-era gates were Ludgate, Newgate, Cripplegate, Bishopsgate and Aldgate, with Aldersgate added around 350; Moorgate is medieval (which makes six Roman-era gates including Newgate) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_Wall]; Newgate lay on the west side of the wall and its road went west over the River Fleet [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newgate]; From the 12th century parts of the gate buildings were used as a gaol [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newgate]; One of the Newgate turrets, which still served as a main gate into the city, was converted into an extension of the prison (the gate buildings and the gaol shared the site) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newgate_Prison]; The gaol later developed into Newgate Prison [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newgate]; Newgate Prison was in use from 1188 to the early twentieth century [SUPPORTED: https://www.londonmuseum.org.uk/collections/london-stories/history-londons-newgate-prison/]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('7ff27a12-f5eb-46e4-b875-310c171a926a'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A "beehive" tholos tomb in the Valley of the Thracian Rulers, where over a thousand royal tombs cluster. Its frescoes are UNESCO-protected.', 'A narrow corridor leads into the domed chamber of the Thracian Tomb of Kazanlak, where a fresco shows a seated couple grasping each other''s wrists as a procession approaches with trays.', 'teaser-card-s014:7ff27a12-f5eb-46e4-b875-310c171a926a:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 185 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '20a544d189cd8e11878855785af8b8bf3d258f7826f9fe033144b2255c3f031b|a81d31363bb570c057224fec1eafea8bb9aeac1a2dea1438800b4950c5d9c60b|a81d31363bb570c057224fec1eafea8bb9aeac1a2dea1438800b4950c5d9c60b', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-015", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "A narrow corridor leads into the domed chamber of the Thracian Tomb of Kazanlak, where a fresco shows a seated couple grasping each other''s wrists as a procession approaches with trays."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-015 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "a narrow corridor leads into a domed chamber [S5]; the tomb is the Thracian Tomb of Kazanlak [S1]; a fresco (mural) in the domed chamber shows a seated couple [S7, S8]; the couple grasp each other''s wrists [S8]; a procession approaches with trays [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-043 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Thracian Tomb of Kazanlak is a tomb (a tholos/beehive tomb) near the town of Kazanlak in Bulgaria [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thracian_Tomb_of_Kazanlak]; A narrow corridor leads into a domed chamber [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thracian_Tomb_of_Kazanlak]; The decoration is a fresco [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thracian_Tomb_of_Kazanlak]; A fresco shows a seated couple grasping each other''s wrists [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thracian_Tomb_of_Kazanlak]; A procession approaches with trays [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thracian_Tomb_of_Kazanlak]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('816d2414-5cf6-457c-9a41-e5ec77d63e72'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A late Pre-Classic city with 80-130 mounds in eight groups, likely Mixe-Zoque. Its carved stelae link Olmec traditions to early Maya art.', 'Near the base of Volcán Tacaná, Izapa has 161 mapped mounds. Its art includes stelae and altars that look like frogs, and most of its monuments are dated to approximately 300 to 50 BCE.', 'teaser-card-s014:816d2414-5cf6-457c-9a41-e5ec77d63e72:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 185 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'aaafa6e8dbe2aa1eab3ea203b0566e6554e1173968dadb2b1dd52afdcee08575|00353d888ceacd5a1c1810da3050c9448c5d452f27ed8d462c240c36440a1616|00353d888ceacd5a1c1810da3050c9448c5d452f27ed8d462c240c36440a1616', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-rewrite1-001", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near the base of Volcán Tacaná, Izapa has 161 mapped mounds. Its art includes stelae and altars that look like frogs, and most of its monuments are dated to approximately 300 to 50 BCE."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check1-001 (check1), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Izapa is near the base of Volcán Tacaná [S2]; 161 mounds have been mapped at Izapa [S4]; its art includes stelae [S6]; its art includes altars that look like frogs [S6]; most of its monuments are dated to approximately 300 to 50 BCE [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-043 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Izapa is a (pre-Columbian) archaeological site in Chiapas, Mexico [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Izapa]; Izapa lies near the base of Volcán Tacaná [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Izapa]; Izapa has 161 mapped mounds [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Izapa]; Izapa''s art includes stelae and altars that look like frogs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Izapa]; Most of Izapa''s monuments are dated to approximately 300 to 50 BCE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Izapa]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('817ff7b7-a814-405a-a5af-0ef173f07045'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The Puente de Alcántara is a Roman arch bridge in Toledo spanning the River Tagus.', 'Below the Castillo de San Servando, the Roman Puente de Alcántara spans the Tagus in Toledo. Its name comes from the Arabic for arch; in 1721 a Baroque arch replaced its eastern tower.', 'teaser-card-s014:817ff7b7-a814-405a-a5af-0ef173f07045:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 184 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'a12635a229784ecf16c6fcbf7d7fa3fca3b758101febd8a445da73b383ab5110|479d7ccf5d0663dbded51bff67a6a911e2619226d8abd1746a5281a191692b51|479d7ccf5d0663dbded51bff67a6a911e2619226d8abd1746a5281a191692b51', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-015", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Below the Castillo de San Servando, the Roman Puente de Alcántara spans the Tagus in Toledo. Its name comes from the Arabic for arch; in 1721 a Baroque arch replaced its eastern tower."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-015 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the bridge lies below (at the foot of) the Castillo de San Servando [S3]; the Puente de Alcántara is a Roman bridge [S1, S3]; it spans the Tagus [S1]; it is in Toledo [S1]; its name comes from the Arabic for arch [S2]; in 1721 a Baroque arch replaced its eastern tower [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-043 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Puente de Alcántara is a bridge in Toledo, Spain (an arch bridge) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puente_de_Alc%C3%A1ntara]; The bridge spans the Tagus [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puente_de_Alc%C3%A1ntara]; The bridge is Roman (built by the Romans) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puente_de_Alc%C3%A1ntara]; The bridge lies below the Castillo de San Servando [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puente_de_Alc%C3%A1ntara]; The name Alcántara comes from Arabic [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puente_de_Alc%C3%A1ntara]; The Arabic word behind the name means arch [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puente_de_Alc%C3%A1ntara]; A Baroque arch replaced the bridge''s eastern tower [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puente_de_Alc%C3%A1ntara]; The replacement of the eastern tower happened in 1721 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puente_de_Alc%C3%A1ntara]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('82ab3246-cd82-4453-bd84-4668d3640ad1'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A Roman castle reused through the Middle Ages, with reliefs of two soldiers and a pregnant woman at its entrance. The "Queen''s Chamber" and dungeons survive.', 'Above the Karakız valley, a rock relief beside the gate of Dağlı Kalesi shows soldiers and a pregnant woman. A highly effaced inscription has so far yielded only a few words.', 'teaser-card-s014:82ab3246-cd82-4453-bd84-4668d3640ad1:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 174 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '196f7b740468f89fba1511c553c2c7ab609f718b703ddeecdb8853f8c8fb659c|2cf241fbc07df7a870773fb6d16bd317cf2d4c715f328f0043b635c34145d422|2cf241fbc07df7a870773fb6d16bd317cf2d4c715f328f0043b635c34145d422', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-015", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Above the Karakız valley, a rock relief beside the gate of Dağlı Kalesi shows soldiers and a pregnant woman. A highly effaced inscription has so far yielded only a few words."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-015 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is above (overlooks) the Karakız valley [S4]; a rock relief lies beside the gate of Dağlı Kalesi [S6]; the relief shows soldiers and a pregnant woman [S6]; there is a highly effaced inscription [S7]; so far only a few words of the inscription have been deciphered (it remains largely undeciphered) [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-043 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Dağlı Kalesi is a castle (ruin) in Mersin Province, Türkiye [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Da%C4%9Fl%C4%B1_Castle]; The castle stands above the Karakız valley [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Da%C4%9Fl%C4%B1_Castle]; A rock relief beside the gate shows soldiers and a pregnant woman [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Da%C4%9Fl%C4%B1_Castle]; The castle has a highly effaced inscription [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Da%C4%9Fl%C4%B1_Castle]; The inscription has so far yielded only a few words [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Da%C4%9Fl%C4%B1_Castle]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('82b9ba8e-bc9a-405a-afe2-fcb210a5d278'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The site with images of llamas, vicuñas, felines, people and the sun lies near Lliwpa Pukyu at a height of 3,449 metres.', 'High in the Apurímac Region, at 3,449 metres, the petroglyphs of Llamachayuq, Quechua for ''with a little llama'', show llamas, vicuñas, felines, people and the sun.', 'teaser-card-s014:82b9ba8e-bc9a-405a-afe2-fcb210a5d278:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 163 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'c85daef6e46025c6225e893371b519123d498a16af545812ddb1c437ab591856|b5c741e0dd1d417077a606781c6cdd1cb03fd3ec3aa26630aa95cf6d7fad2a19|b5c741e0dd1d417077a606781c6cdd1cb03fd3ec3aa26630aa95cf6d7fad2a19', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-015", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "High in the Apurímac Region, at 3,449 metres, the petroglyphs of Llamachayuq, Quechua for ''with a little llama'', show llamas, vicuñas, felines, people and the sun."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-015 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is in the Apurímac Region [S2]; it lies at a height of 3,449 metres [S3]; the site has petroglyphs [S1]; Llamachayuq is Quechua for ''with a little llama'' [S1]; the images show llamas, vicuñas, felines, people and the sun [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-043 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Llamachayuq is an archaeological complex with petroglyphs in Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Llamachayuq]; Llamachayuq is located in the Apurímac Region [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Llamachayuq]; The site lies at a height of 3,449 metres [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Llamachayuq]; Llamachayuq is Quechua for ''with a little llama'' [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Llamachayuq]; The petroglyphs show llamas, vicuñas, felines, people and the sun [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Llamachayuq]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('831371bc-5719-4399-ac7b-d0defb2022b0'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The site is situated some 250 metres above sea level overlooking a series of valleys and down to the Exe Valley.', 'Some 250 metres above sea level, the Iron Age hillfort of Cadbury Castle looks down to the Exe Valley. Here Thomas Fairfax''s Parliamentarians camped while besieging Bickleigh Castle.', 'teaser-card-s014:831371bc-5719-4399-ac7b-d0defb2022b0:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 182 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '5f7dc4a7dfa3936ce289532d610e8b800be74ffa80dea09d1db00d220abde60d|0771f1992fcd72811e97c83c7e1023bfba6e38c0568d2ea3447b6490b49061c8|0771f1992fcd72811e97c83c7e1023bfba6e38c0568d2ea3447b6490b49061c8', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-015", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Some 250 metres above sea level, the Iron Age hillfort of Cadbury Castle looks down to the Exe Valley. Here Thomas Fairfax''s Parliamentarians camped while besieging Bickleigh Castle."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-015 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is some 250 metres above sea level [S3]; Cadbury Castle is an Iron Age hillfort [S1]; it looks down to the Exe Valley [S3]; Thomas Fairfax''s Parliamentarians camped here [S2]; they camped while besieging Bickleigh Castle [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-044 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cadbury Castle (Devon, England, near Bickleigh) is an Iron Age hillfort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadbury_Castle,_Devon]; The site lies some 250 metres above sea level [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadbury_Castle,_Devon]; It looks down to the Exe Valley [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadbury_Castle,_Devon]; Thomas Fairfax''s Parliamentarian forces camped at the hillfort during the English Civil War [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadbury_Castle,_Devon]; They camped there while besieging Bickleigh Castle [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadbury_Castle,_Devon]; The siege of Bickleigh Castle was laid from Cadbury (Fairfax''s Parliamentarian troops attacked the castle) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bickleigh_Castle]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('83711b0a-71a6-4022-88f9-060aada84ea6'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A Neolithic court tomb also called the "Giant''s Grave." Court tombs get their name from the open-air courtyard where mourners gathered before entering the burial chambers.', 'On the shore of Carlingford Lough, with no surface features visible, a 1976 dig revealed Ballinran Court Tomb: a forecourt facing the Mourne Mountains, a nest of winkle shells in a pit.', 'teaser-card-s014:83711b0a-71a6-4022-88f9-060aada84ea6:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 185 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '94f9118b00082d55064e2f087e0516edcacccce0ef818846ff5b709ba16365d8|424f60c8591aa713fe810b8a898d8b61291f7ed8c1723ee6c61cb31fc3f7146d|424f60c8591aa713fe810b8a898d8b61291f7ed8c1723ee6c61cb31fc3f7146d', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-015", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the shore of Carlingford Lough, with no surface features visible, a 1976 dig revealed Ballinran Court Tomb: a forecourt facing the Mourne Mountains, a nest of winkle shells in a pit."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-015 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is on the shore of Carlingford Lough [S1]; no surface features were visible [S3]; a dig in 1976 revealed the court tomb [S2, S3]; the forecourt faces the Mourne Mountains [S5]; a ''nest'' of winkle shells was found in a pit [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-044 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ballinran Court Tomb, a court tomb in Northern Ireland (County Down), lies on the shore of Carlingford Lough [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballinran_Court_Tomb]; No surface features were visible before the dig, yet it revealed a court tomb [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballinran_Court_Tomb]; The tomb was revealed by a dig in 1976 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballinran_Court_Tomb]; The forecourt faces the Mourne Mountains [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballinran_Court_Tomb]; A nest of winkle shells was placed in a pit in the grave [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballinran_Court_Tomb]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('83cbd9c5-1962-4f95-8f49-eeb525af234f'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The twelfth fort on Hadrian''s Wall, guarding an important approach from the Bewcastle area. It sat on a high bluff commanding the Cambeck Valley.', 'From a high bluff over the Cambeck Valley, Camboglanna, a Roman fort on Hadrian''s Wall, watched for raiders from Bewcastle. In 1791 the gardens of Castlesteads House were laid over it.', 'teaser-card-s014:83cbd9c5-1962-4f95-8f49-eeb525af234f:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 184 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '1be917f2b6b34aa670efbab9af64d8e1bed24498df2ff5a12c7f3af08d103ce5|54c74df73e02f6278d34db2bd2af1236628ad3850f0e37f7729d153a4e1050ce|54c74df73e02f6278d34db2bd2af1236628ad3850f0e37f7729d153a4e1050ce', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-015", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "From a high bluff over the Cambeck Valley, Camboglanna, a Roman fort on Hadrian''s Wall, watched for raiders from Bewcastle. In 1791 the gardens of Castlesteads House were laid over it."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-015 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the fort stood on a high bluff over the Cambeck Valley [S3]; Camboglanna was a Roman fort [S1]; it was on Hadrian''s Wall [S2]; it watched for raiders from the Bewcastle area [S4]; in 1791 the gardens of Castlesteads House were laid over the site [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-044 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Camboglanna (modern Castlesteads) was a Roman fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camboglanna]; The fort stood on Hadrian''s Wall (the twelfth fort counting from the east) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camboglanna]; The fort stood on a high bluff over the Cambeck Valley [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camboglanna]; It watched for raiders from the Bewcastle area [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camboglanna]; The gardens of Castlesteads House were laid over the fort site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camboglanna]; This happened in 1791 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camboglanna]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8442fc68-e408-4b2f-9aad-a5f42139685e'::uuid, 'card_description', '5th-millennium BC Indus Civilisation trading post in Balochistan, mound 457 m across — a major regional centre. Finds include stone beads, copper pieces, and figurines with Harappan connections.', 'In Balochistan rises Dabarkot, an Indus Civilization mound 500 yards in diameter, where excavators recovered stone beads, copper pieces, a gold pin and figurines with a Harappan connection.', 'teaser-card-s014:8442fc68-e408-4b2f-9aad-a5f42139685e:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 189 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '58d7e2f77723aefa6256a6a712fbb80b5b51e3d5eec28717cb3ae0e3da650d16|c893f61b8275c9f7a9472642f5ac13f1688f4d6c9c2efe6ff682c54be41f333b|c893f61b8275c9f7a9472642f5ac13f1688f4d6c9c2efe6ff682c54be41f333b', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-015", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In Balochistan rises Dabarkot, an Indus Civilization mound 500 yards in diameter, where excavators recovered stone beads, copper pieces, a gold pin and figurines with a Harappan connection."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-015 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Dabarkot is in Balochistan [S1]; it is an Indus Civilization mound (tell) [S1, S4]; the mound is 500 yards in diameter [S4]; excavators recovered stone beads, copper pieces and a gold pin [S5]; excavated figurines show a Harappan connection [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-044 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Dabarkot is a mound (tell) site of the Indus Civilization [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dabarkot]; It is in Balochistan (Pakistan) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dabarkot]; The mound is 500 yards in diameter [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dabarkot]; Excavators recovered stone beads [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dabarkot]; Excavators recovered copper pieces [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dabarkot]; Excavators recovered a gold pin [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dabarkot]; Figurines from the site show a Harappan connection [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dabarkot]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8466f428-5e7d-4d8b-bf67-68cc758c6f01'::uuid, 'card_description', NULL, 'In a valley of the Puuc Hills, directly between Labná and Sayil, the small Maya site of Xlapak keeps its decorations well preserved, among them masks of the Yucatec Maya rain god Chaac.', 'teaser-card-s014:8466f428-5e7d-4d8b-bf67-68cc758c6f01:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 185 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '5da6fb9ff34bb216aae7fd923a728d1fd9b3fc74e60ee13f417c7dc3afc5cb2b|dca181d7756b19125a34eed51266df500b0c538e7cc4277c9d7179c7f3f9f463|dca181d7756b19125a34eed51266df500b0c538e7cc4277c9d7179c7f3f9f463', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-015", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In a valley of the Puuc Hills, directly between Labná and Sayil, the small Maya site of Xlapak keeps its decorations well preserved, among them masks of the Yucatec Maya rain god Chaac."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-015 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site lies in a valley of the Puuc Hills [S3]; it lies directly between Labná and Sayil [S2]; Xlapak is a small Maya site [S1]; its decorations are well preserved [S8]; the decorations include masks of the Yucatec Maya rain god Chaac [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-044 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Xlapak is a small Maya archaeological site in Yucatan, Mexico [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xlapak]; It lies in a valley of the Puuc Hills [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xlapak]; It lies directly between Labná and Sayil [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xlapak]; Labná and Sayil are the two neighbouring sites (Xlapak is about 4 km from Labná) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xlapak]; The decorations at Xlapak are well preserved [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xlapak]; The decorations include masks of Chaac [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xlapak]; Chaac is the Yucatec Maya rain god [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xlapak]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8541ba4b-43ff-4428-b155-8e12fb96dafc'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A Romano-Celtic temple with column capitals sculpted as heads of the Sun and Moon. Rich finds of glass, coins, and terracotta antefixes indicate a wealthy sanctuary.', 'In Aurillac, the Romano-Celtic temple of Fanum d''Aron rings a circular chamber with a polygonal gallery opening onto the Cère and Jordanne valleys, in use from the 1st to 3rd centuries CE.', 'teaser-card-s014:8541ba4b-43ff-4428-b155-8e12fb96dafc:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 188 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '15b90020b6ee34270283232315949fcb6b8060ebfc493b448ca979b01d2d6de1|562566e9ab69ef4d3c5e748d7f629788542f6beabb156ce67a3ac8911a42b3f7|562566e9ab69ef4d3c5e748d7f629788542f6beabb156ce67a3ac8911a42b3f7', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-015", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In Aurillac, the Romano-Celtic temple of Fanum d''Aron rings a circular chamber with a polygonal gallery opening onto the Cère and Jordanne valleys, in use from the 1st to 3rd centuries CE."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-015 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is in Aurillac [S1]; it is a Romano-Celtic temple [S1]; it has a circular chamber surrounded by a polygonal gallery [S5]; the gallery opens onto the Cère and Jordanne valleys [S5]; the temple was in use from the 1st to 3rd centuries CE [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-045 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Fanum d''Aron is a temple [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fanum_d''Aron]; The temple is Romano-Celtic in type [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fanum_d''Aron]; The temple is in Aurillac (France) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fanum_d''Aron]; The temple has a circular chamber (cella) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fanum_d''Aron]; The circular chamber is ringed by a polygonal gallery [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fanum_d''Aron]; The gallery opens onto the Cère valley [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fanum_d''Aron]; The gallery opens onto the Jordanne valley [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fanum_d''Aron]; The temple was in use from the 1st century CE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fanum_d''Aron]; The temple was in use until the 3rd century CE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fanum_d''Aron]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('85a6ae2e-6f62-44f2-9dfd-fd81b229d56d'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Yazılıkaya was a sanctuary of Hattusa, the capital city of the Hittite Empire, today in the Çorum Province.', 'Yazılıkkaya, the inscribed rock, was a sanctuary of Hattusa, the Hittite capital. Its rock reliefs portray the gods of the Hittite pantheon; its uses may have included New Year ceremonies.', 'teaser-card-s014:85a6ae2e-6f62-44f2-9dfd-fd81b229d56d:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 188 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'f16618198f4c3542cbbdcbeab93d6c1903e339c1800cff87465125cf878f3910|2f1bccac73e27b73ec391a3899445ea8fdeae7e215e48ed8e606a05178c77cfc|2f1bccac73e27b73ec391a3899445ea8fdeae7e215e48ed8e606a05178c77cfc', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-015", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Yazılıkkaya, the inscribed rock, was a sanctuary of Hattusa, the Hittite capital. Its rock reliefs portray the gods of the Hittite pantheon; its uses may have included New Year ceremonies."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-015 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the name means ''the inscribed rock'' [S1]; it was a sanctuary of Hattusa [S1]; Hattusa was the Hittite capital [S1]; its rock reliefs portray the gods of the Hittite pantheon [S2]; its uses may have included New Year ceremonies (a hedge, kept as a hedge) [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-045 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Yazılıkaya was a sanctuary [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yaz%C4%B1l%C4%B1kaya]; The sanctuary belonged to Hattusa [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yaz%C4%B1l%C4%B1kaya]; Hattusa was the Hittite capital [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yaz%C4%B1l%C4%B1kaya]; The name Yazılıkaya means the inscribed rock [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yaz%C4%B1l%C4%B1kaya]; The site has rock reliefs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yaz%C4%B1l%C4%B1kaya]; The rock reliefs portray the gods of the Hittite pantheon [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yaz%C4%B1l%C4%B1kaya]; Its uses may have included New Year ceremonies (stated as a possibility, not a certainty) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yaz%C4%B1l%C4%B1kaya]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('85afeb46-025d-4325-a4df-b8a45a2fe008'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A Roman villa occupied 140–400 AD as the center of a farming estate. At its largest it had multiple heated rooms — then was completely abandoned and forgotten.', 'In 1926 workmen preparing a driveway unearthed Roman artefacts and so found Crofton Roman Villa, inhabited between approximately 140 and 400 AD, whose hypocaust can still be seen.', 'teaser-card-s014:85afeb46-025d-4325-a4df-b8a45a2fe008:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 179 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '31d2ee55a286ca54f67e68c5db1a08b17cd56ce6247e6713f488b3c8e1daeab1|cb9fa986cfd91ed7ba74eebbd7ee1e8a34393d8d07863363442a5e26c1e05afc|cb9fa986cfd91ed7ba74eebbd7ee1e8a34393d8d07863363442a5e26c1e05afc', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-015", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In 1926 workmen preparing a driveway unearthed Roman artefacts and so found Crofton Roman Villa, inhabited between approximately 140 and 400 AD, whose hypocaust can still be seen."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-015 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "in 1926 workmen were preparing a driveway [S6]; the workmen unearthed Roman artefacts [S6]; this led to the discovery of Crofton Roman Villa [S6, S7]; the villa was inhabited between approximately 140 and 400 AD [S1]; its hypocaust can be seen [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-045 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Crofton Roman Villa is a Roman villa [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crofton_Roman_Villa]; The villa is in England (Crofton, Orpington, London Borough of Bromley) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crofton_Roman_Villa]; The site was found in 1926 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crofton_Roman_Villa]; Workmen preparing a driveway found it [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crofton_Roman_Villa]; The workmen unearthed Roman artefacts, which led to the villa''s discovery [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crofton_Roman_Villa]; The villa was inhabited from approximately 140 AD [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crofton_Roman_Villa]; The villa was inhabited until approximately 400 AD [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crofton_Roman_Villa]; The villa has a hypocaust [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crofton_Roman_Villa]; The hypocaust can still be seen today [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crofton_Roman_Villa]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('861136bb-c3b6-4cf6-8e0c-ff756d744c12'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Leading to the burial chamber, the corridor is covered with a smaller boulder, supported by four stones, whose upper surface has more than 180 bowl-shaped carvings dating from the Bronze Age.', 'On the island of Møn, Sømarkedyssen, a Neolithic tomb from around 3400 BC, holds an octagonal chamber, and the boulder over its corridor bears more than 180 bowl-shaped Bronze Age carvings.', 'teaser-card-s014:861136bb-c3b6-4cf6-8e0c-ff756d744c12:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 189 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '4126ecfe64ba57653a03628fd0537c03a3c1c3e60efbe40e0bab519ec4b210bc|a7f090f46e8872b8261b0fd2b4e619bdc3210daa327d3191a221849508c95cc8|a7f090f46e8872b8261b0fd2b4e619bdc3210daa327d3191a221849508c95cc8', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-015", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the island of Møn, Sømarkedyssen, a Neolithic tomb from around 3400 BC, holds an octagonal chamber, and the boulder over its corridor bears more than 180 bowl-shaped Bronze Age carvings."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-015 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is on the island of Møn [S1]; it is a Neolithic tomb [S1]; it dates from around 3400 BC [S2]; it has an octagonal chamber [S2]; a boulder covers its corridor [S4]; the boulder bears more than 180 bowl-shaped carvings [S4]; the carvings date from the Bronze Age [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-045 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Sømarkedyssen is a tomb [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S%C3%B8markedyssen]; The tomb is on the island of Møn (Denmark) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S%C3%B8markedyssen]; The tomb is Neolithic [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S%C3%B8markedyssen]; The tomb dates from around 3400 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S%C3%B8markedyssen]; The tomb holds an octagonal chamber [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S%C3%B8markedyssen]; A boulder lies over the tomb''s corridor [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S%C3%B8markedyssen]; The boulder bears more than 180 carvings [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S%C3%B8markedyssen]; The carvings are bowl-shaped [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S%C3%B8markedyssen]; The carvings date from the Bronze Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S%C3%B8markedyssen]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('86207cd7-b3f0-45e0-b589-97f8a04cc300'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The site was excavated between 31 December 1957 and 3 January 1958 by Peter Pool and Vivien Russell which found the stone had been erected in an elliptical pit and wedged in place using small stones.', 'In Penwith, Carfury Standing Stone, also called Cuckoo Rock, stands around 3m tall, wedged with small stones in an elliptical pit. Downhill, Vivien Russell noted a potential sister stone.', 'teaser-card-s014:86207cd7-b3f0-45e0-b589-97f8a04cc300:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 187 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '0fa42593707204b6123f17ca721c750baf0babbe00ccc41c621a8ca11f63dd62|8b9a90418b7bd7d3d7d16634efdb6bbaa4967a90016e40da6963f5817b4a59aa|8b9a90418b7bd7d3d7d16634efdb6bbaa4967a90016e40da6963f5817b4a59aa', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-015", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In Penwith, Carfury Standing Stone, also called Cuckoo Rock, stands around 3m tall, wedged with small stones in an elliptical pit. Downhill, Vivien Russell noted a potential sister stone."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-015 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is in Penwith [S1]; Carfury Standing Stone is also called Cuckoo Rock [S2]; the stone stands around 3m tall [S2]; it was wedged with small stones [S3]; it was set in an elliptical pit [S3]; Vivien Russell noted a potential sister stone downhill (a hedge, kept as a hedge) [S3, S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-045 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Carfury Standing Stone is a standing stone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carfury_Standing_Stone]; The stone is in Penwith (Cornwall, England) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carfury_Standing_Stone]; The stone is also called Cuckoo Rock [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carfury_Standing_Stone]; The stone stands around 3m tall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carfury_Standing_Stone]; The stone is set in an elliptical pit [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carfury_Standing_Stone]; The stone is wedged in place with small stones [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carfury_Standing_Stone]; Vivien Russell noted a potential sister stone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carfury_Standing_Stone]; The potential sister stone lies downhill from the standing stone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carfury_Standing_Stone]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('86b67ab6-879c-4b80-a6ab-7bae209b8dc4'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A rock shelter in El Cogul, Catalonia, bearing Levantine rock art and schematic art from two distinct prehistoric traditions. Latin inscriptions confirm its use as a sanctuary into historical times.', 'In 1908 the village rector found them: figures in bright red, black and dark red in the rock shelter of Roca dels Moros, where inscriptions tell of a sanctuary into Iberian and Roman times.', 'teaser-card-s014:86b67ab6-879c-4b80-a6ab-7bae209b8dc4:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 189 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '8f740fd09114ea12495231f1d07379e138310708a75df3a2764a10b0c0edc9c5|d799cf71609a81237207c7edfe3873380703447886917f6babc3bb6e0ceb86e8|d799cf71609a81237207c7edfe3873380703447886917f6babc3bb6e0ceb86e8', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In 1908 the village rector found them: figures in bright red, black and dark red in the rock shelter of Roca dels Moros, where inscriptions tell of a sanctuary into Iberian and Roman times."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is the rock shelter of Roca dels Moros [S1]; the paintings were found in 1908 [S5]; they were found by the village rector [S5]; the figures are painted in bright red, black and dark red [S6]; inscriptions at the place indicate it was a sanctuary [S4]; the sanctuary use lasted into Iberian and Roman times [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-verify-046 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Roca dels Moros is a rock shelter with prehistoric rock paintings (the central claim: what kind of place it is) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roca_dels_Moros]; The site is in El Cogul, Catalonia, Spain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roca_dels_Moros]; The paintings were found in 1908 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roca_dels_Moros]; The finder was the village rector (Ramon Huguet of El Cogul) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roca_dels_Moros]; The figures are painted in bright red, black and dark red [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roca_dels_Moros]; Inscriptions at the shelter indicate a sanctuary that was used into Iberian and Roman times [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roca_dels_Moros]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('87033d8b-bb1e-48c4-a743-7dde02b2db33'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A Maya site in northern Petén active c. 900 BC–1200 AD, La Blanca features a large acropolis in the Mopan River valley. Its Middle Preclassic core predates most regional ceremonial centres.', 'At Sitio Arqueológico La Blanca, a Maya site, graffiti is inscribed on the acropolis''s stucco walls, and its formal occupation appears to have ended violently, with evidence of a battle.', 'teaser-card-s014:87033d8b-bb1e-48c4-a743-7dde02b2db33:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 186 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '5527493a5c78a4e953356095ffdaf3d68abd1f0e021fc07a8cebe78e4cb5b515|62b58110206ab62d1c373568f1f95aa591e9e1dc1b3aa4265f5e7f8d6e80528f|62b58110206ab62d1c373568f1f95aa591e9e1dc1b3aa4265f5e7f8d6e80528f', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-rewrite1-001", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At Sitio Arqueológico La Blanca, a Maya site, graffiti is inscribed on the acropolis''s stucco walls, and its formal occupation appears to have ended violently, with evidence of a battle."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check1-001 (check1), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is a Maya site [S1]; graffiti is inscribed on the stucco walls of the acropolis [S7]; the formal occupation of the city appears to have ended violently (hedged) [S4]; there is evidence of a battle [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-verify-046 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "La Blanca (Sitio Arqueologico La Blanca, Peten) is a Maya archaeological site in Guatemala (the central claim: what kind of place it is) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Blanca,_Peten]; The site has an acropolis [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Blanca,_Peten]; Graffiti is inscribed on the stucco walls of the acropolis [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Blanca,_Peten]; The end of the formal occupation of the site appears to have been violent [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Blanca,_Peten]; There is evidence of a battle, recovered in the excavations of the acropolis [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Blanca,_Peten]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8793ca33-397e-47a5-a6bb-020802f49e7c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Over 170 round-house traces, 100 still visible on this Llŷn Peninsula hillfort. The lower rampart is the original defence; a second eastern rampart was added in the 6th or 7th century AD.', 'Behind the Iron Age stone rampart of Garn Boduan, a hillfort near Nefyn, around 100 round houses are still visible on the ground, home perhaps to between 100 and 400 people.', 'teaser-card-s014:8793ca33-397e-47a5-a6bb-020802f49e7c:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 173 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '11ee948a22ad0bc52616a02066d27324fc5dc67291635cbac1a56bee19cdce6f|5582a5e3cd545ad779528c1425c4bd3ad68a3c39c1736bececa92b337cd91279|5582a5e3cd545ad779528c1425c4bd3ad68a3c39c1736bececa92b337cd91279', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Behind the Iron Age stone rampart of Garn Boduan, a hillfort near Nefyn, around 100 round houses are still visible on the ground, home perhaps to between 100 and 400 people."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Garn Boduan is a hillfort [S1]; it is near Nefyn [S1]; it has an Iron Age stone rampart [S4]; the round houses lie inside the fort, behind that rampart [S2, S3, S4]; around 100 round houses are still visible on the ground [S2]; perhaps between 100 and 400 people lived there [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-verify-046 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Garn Boduan is a hillfort (the central claim: what kind of place it is) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garn_Boduan]; The hillfort is near Nefyn (Llyn Peninsula, Wales) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garn_Boduan]; The hillfort dates to the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garn_Boduan]; The fort has a stone rampart built in the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garn_Boduan]; Around 100 round houses are still visible on the ground [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garn_Boduan]; Round houses stand on the site of the fort: a 1950s survey identified traces of over 170 of them [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garn_Boduan]; Perhaps between 100 and 400 people lived in the fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garn_Boduan]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('87cc7367-7c4d-4b5b-ae73-9cf41c550e9c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The Knap of Howar on the island of Papa Westray in Orkney is a Neolithic farmstead which may be the oldest preserved stone house in northern Europe.', 'On Papa Westray, the Knap of Howar may be the oldest preserved stone house in northern Europe: a Neolithic farmstead lived in from 3800 BC to 2800 BC, its low doorways facing the sea.', 'teaser-card-s014:87cc7367-7c4d-4b5b-ae73-9cf41c550e9c:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 183 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '3ad3f2fc2d84b16c76fe526d478a3456af8b2d81413ca71ee0f21fe87d4c1429|dd839ae11cda85cd0a2056822b54e158bcb68c3f4bf993c52ed3d31032239ea4|dd839ae11cda85cd0a2056822b54e158bcb68c3f4bf993c52ed3d31032239ea4', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On Papa Westray, the Knap of Howar may be the oldest preserved stone house in northern Europe: a Neolithic farmstead lived in from 3800 BC to 2800 BC, its low doorways facing the sea."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Knap of Howar is on Papa Westray [S1]; it may be the oldest preserved stone house in northern Europe [S1]; it is a Neolithic farmstead [S1]; it was lived in (occupied) from 3800 BC to 2800 BC [S2]; its doorways are low and face the sea [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-verify-046 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Knap of Howar is a Neolithic farmstead (the central claim: what kind of place it is) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knap_of_Howar]; It is on Papa Westray (Orkney, Scotland) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knap_of_Howar]; It may be the oldest preserved stone house in northern Europe [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knap_of_Howar]; It was lived in from 3800 BC to 2800 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knap_of_Howar]; Its doorways are low and face the sea [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knap_of_Howar]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('87fec19f-7aa6-42e9-9aba-bfdf3c3938d6'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the mountain outside the town of Roscigno is the archaeological site of Monte Pruno, a settlement of the Oenotrians and the Lucani.', 'High in the southern Cilento, on a mountain outside the town of Roscigno, lies Monte Pruno: a settlement of the Oenotrians and the Lucani, home to them from the 7th to the 3rd centuries BCE.', 'teaser-card-s014:87fec19f-7aa6-42e9-9aba-bfdf3c3938d6:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 190 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '684520eabf90c058ce9fab94683090fc73950e09f2ed371c17ae39c4b302f578|2a044f0459d22d8d396cb38dc6caf40e797480a2eca413cc4d908d7738c41e65|2a044f0459d22d8d396cb38dc6caf40e797480a2eca413cc4d908d7738c41e65', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "High in the southern Cilento, on a mountain outside the town of Roscigno, lies Monte Pruno: a settlement of the Oenotrians and the Lucani, home to them from the 7th to the 3rd centuries BCE."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Monte Pruno lies in the southern Cilento [S1]; it is on a mountain (high) [S1, S3]; the mountain is outside the town of Roscigno [S3]; the site is a settlement of the Oenotrians and the Lucani [S3]; the settlement dates from the 7th to the 3rd centuries BCE [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-verify-046 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Monte Pruno is an archaeological site, a settlement (the central claim: what kind of place it is), on a mountain outside the town of Roscigno [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monte_Pruno]; Monte Pruno lies in the southern Cilento [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monte_Pruno]; The settlement was one of the Oenotrians and the Lucani [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monte_Pruno]; The settlement dates from the 7th to the 3rd centuries BCE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monte_Pruno]; The mountain is high (879 m above sea level) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monte_Pruno]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('884eefd5-fec7-49da-972f-dfd3a8c099ab'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The stone circle is located on Bocan Hill, just outside the village of Culdaff on the Inishowen peninsula.', 'In a heather-covered field on Bocan Hill, two tall stones seem to mark an entrance to the Bocan Stone Circle. Of around 30 stones, some up to 2 metres tall, only seven still stand.', 'teaser-card-s014:884eefd5-fec7-49da-972f-dfd3a8c099ab:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 180 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'e7c61f1322e54b815c3b85ae748a92790159ec400cdb4a09f0e21c9593dbce9a|b493d667ec001302437f73d17b5321e6260c636a74d08d9c10c6b8ddd9f92e4f|b493d667ec001302437f73d17b5321e6260c636a74d08d9c10c6b8ddd9f92e4f', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In a heather-covered field on Bocan Hill, two tall stones seem to mark an entrance to the Bocan Stone Circle. Of around 30 stones, some up to 2 metres tall, only seven still stand."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the circle lies in a heather-covered field [S3]; the circle is on Bocan Hill [S2]; two tall stones seem to mark an entrance [S7]; there were originally around 30 stones [S6]; some stones are up to 2 metres tall [S6]; only seven stones still stand [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-047 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Bocan Stone Circle is a stone circle, situated near Culdaff on the Inishowen peninsula, County Donegal, Ireland (central claim: what the site is and where) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bocan_Stone_Circle]; The stone circle stands on Bocan Hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bocan_Stone_Circle]; The circle lies in a heather-covered field [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bocan_Stone_Circle]; Two tall stones seem to mark an entrance to the circle [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bocan_Stone_Circle]; The circle originally had around 30 stones [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bocan_Stone_Circle]; Some of the stones were up to 2 metres tall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bocan_Stone_Circle]; Only seven stones still stand [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bocan_Stone_Circle]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('885ee465-142a-4368-a08c-66295e94cfab'::uuid, 'card_description', 'An Aztec ceremonial site now surrounded by the modern city of Cuernavaca. Its dual-staircase pyramid follows the classic Aztec double-temple design.', 'Built on a hill formed from a lava flow, Zona Arqueológica Teopanzolco has only its ceremonial centre preserved. The Spanish Conquest in 1521 closed its prehispanic history.', 'teaser-card-s014:885ee465-142a-4368-a08c-66295e94cfab:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 173 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'a3bd27051654e3fe3b08199b7ee65387007d56bd9b95444ce9c71fa643cf71b1|74048f88446839969eca24f44dc95d6bc256cf65cdcb381ad18fe52b8b1477a4|74048f88446839969eca24f44dc95d6bc256cf65cdcb381ad18fe52b8b1477a4', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-rewrite1-001", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Built on a hill formed from a lava flow, Zona Arqueológica Teopanzolco has only its ceremonial centre preserved. The Spanish Conquest in 1521 closed its prehispanic history."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check1-001 (check1), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Teopanzolco was built on a hill formed from a lava flow [S3]; only its ceremonial centre has been preserved [S5]; the Spanish Conquest in 1521 closed its prehispanic history [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-047 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Teopanzolco (Zona Arqueologica Teopanzolco) is an archaeological site in the Mexican state of Morelos, now within Cuernavaca (central claim: what the site is and where) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teopanzolco]; The site was built on a hill formed from a lava flow [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teopanzolco]; Only the ceremonial centre of the site has been preserved [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teopanzolco]; The Spanish Conquest in 1521 closed the prehispanic history of the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teopanzolco]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('89bb7456-d47b-4771-8d89-a14827233844'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The site was settled for about 500 years, starting sometime between circa 2800 and 2100 BC.', 'Just 2 miles north-east of Stonehenge, a large timber circle rose at Durrington Walls circa 2600 BC, turned southeast to face the sunrise on the midwinter solstice.', 'teaser-card-s014:89bb7456-d47b-4771-8d89-a14827233844:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 164 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '596908c5b2c2b96783d7c1b5546b643dcfae31c1f8911863785b7ef9d04da360|cb07d35d58120b8c203677034a6c1be853bd80014526398cddfe0ad344b5efe1|cb07d35d58120b8c203677034a6c1be853bd80014526398cddfe0ad344b5efe1', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Just 2 miles north-east of Stonehenge, a large timber circle rose at Durrington Walls circa 2600 BC, turned southeast to face the sunrise on the midwinter solstice."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Durrington Walls lies 2 miles north-east of Stonehenge [S2]; a large timber circle was built at the site [S5]; it was built circa 2600 BC [S5]; the circle was oriented southeast towards the midwinter solstice sunrise [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-047 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Durrington Walls is a Neolithic settlement and henge enclosure with a large timber circle, in the Stonehenge World Heritage Site in England (central claim: what the site is and where) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Durrington_Walls]; Durrington Walls lies 2 miles north-east of Stonehenge [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Durrington_Walls]; A large timber circle was built at Durrington Walls around 2600 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Durrington_Walls]; The timber circle was oriented (turned) to the southeast [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Durrington_Walls]; The timber circle faced the sunrise on the midwinter solstice [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Durrington_Walls]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8a87a57e-4e29-42e7-9dca-08f3c6c16539'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Iron Age hillfort in Dorset enclosing 3.6 hectares, with a rampart 7 m wide and an external ditch 13 m wide. Active 2nd millennium BC into the 1st century AD — a Scheduled Monument on a low hill.', 'When Fitzwalter Foy cleared the woods from Dungeon Hill in the late 18th century, this Iron Age hillfort in Dorset gave up human bones, sword blades and Roman coins.', 'teaser-card-s014:8a87a57e-4e29-42e7-9dca-08f3c6c16539:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 165 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '7ca34c7e9061000722a004dc66d0f5e29454a110b671585b0b8668a9da4addd6|ac9fb4801649c9bfefa8d9423f35f59f4eefc424ea5473cc4bc1943c2a175591|ac9fb4801649c9bfefa8d9423f35f59f4eefc424ea5473cc4bc1943c2a175591', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "When Fitzwalter Foy cleared the woods from Dungeon Hill in the late 18th century, this Iron Age hillfort in Dorset gave up human bones, sword blades and Roman coins."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Fitzwalter Foy cleared the woodland from Dungeon Hill [S6]; he did so in the late 18th century [S6]; Dungeon Hill is an Iron Age hillfort in Dorset [S1]; human bones were recovered [S6]; sword blades were recovered [S6]; Roman coins were recovered [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-047 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Dungeon Hill is an Iron Age hillfort (central claim: what the site is) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dungeon_Hill]; The hillfort lies in Dorset (near Buckland Newton), England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dungeon_Hill]; The owner of the fort was named Fitzwalter Foy [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dungeon_Hill]; In the late 18th century Foy cleared the woods (woodland) from the hill fort [SUPPORTED: https://heritage.dorsetcouncil.gov.uk/Monument/MDO567]; Human bones were recovered when the woodland was cleared [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dungeon_Hill]; Sword blades were recovered when the woodland was cleared [SUPPORTED: https://heritage.dorsetcouncil.gov.uk/Monument/MDO567]; Roman coins were recovered when the woodland was cleared [SUPPORTED: https://heritage.dorsetcouncil.gov.uk/Monument/MDO567]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8b1bec65-9c91-4ba1-a8c9-ccde8579e04c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The Stadium of Delphi lies on the highest spot of the Archaeological Site of Delphi.', 'On the highest spot of Delphi, above the sanctuary of Apollo, lies the Stadium of Delphi, the best preserved ancient stadium in Greece, where the track and field contests were held.', 'teaser-card-s014:8b1bec65-9c91-4ba1-a8c9-ccde8579e04c:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 181 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '9e707a651ff4569a57fefdfe0cb587d8e00174e0856b831e7486faaca884f3bf|585269b861f1315476e41610398dbffcf83afd0af73b7cbf47f9a67710cb7d68|585269b861f1315476e41610398dbffcf83afd0af73b7cbf47f9a67710cb7d68', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the highest spot of Delphi, above the sanctuary of Apollo, lies the Stadium of Delphi, the best preserved ancient stadium in Greece, where the track and field contests were held."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the stadium lies on the highest spot of the site of Delphi [S1, S5]; it lies above (overlooks) the sanctuary of Apollo [S2, S5]; it is the best preserved ancient stadium in Greece [S4]; the track and field contests were held there [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-047 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Stadium of Delphi is an ancient stadium at the archaeological site of Delphi, Greece (central claim: what the site is and where) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stadium_of_Delphi]; The stadium lies on the highest spot of Delphi [SUPPORTED: https://delphi.culture.gr/stadium/]; The stadium lies above (overlooks) the sanctuary of Apollo [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stadium_of_Delphi]; The Stadium of Delphi is the best preserved ancient stadium in Greece [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stadium_of_Delphi]; The track and field contests were held in the stadium [SUPPORTED: https://delphi.culture.gr/stadium/]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8b78f040-2e72-4754-b3e1-0b78a4e970c0'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Archaeological finds from the area, including the Gevninge helmet fragment, show evidence of occupation during the Iron Age and Viking Age, from approximately 500 AD to 1000 AD.', 'Around Gevninge, finds such as a helmet fragment tell of life from approximately 500 AD to 1000 AD, 3 kilometres north of Lejre, believed to have been the seat of the Scylding kings.', 'teaser-card-s014:8b78f040-2e72-4754-b3e1-0b78a4e970c0:card_description', 'teaser-card-s014 (teaser-card): teaser card, 182 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '7a955263d7cc63f9df501df68827898873f6b280b1fa9e32fd25eb19dc0a1731|e8ab3f21e7e4882f01a1c4a882f19b7e009716de8380941b611bdba0db2963bc|e8ab3f21e7e4882f01a1c4a882f19b7e009716de8380941b611bdba0db2963bc', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Around Gevninge, finds such as a helmet fragment tell of life from approximately 500 AD to 1000 AD, 3 kilometres north of Lejre, believed to have been the seat of the Scylding kings."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "finds from around Gevninge include a helmet fragment [S3]; the finds show evidence of occupation (life) from approximately 500 AD to 1000 AD [S3]; Gevninge is 3 kilometres north of Lejre [S4]; Lejre is believed to have been the seat of the Scylding kings [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-048 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Gevninge is a settlement (a small town) in Lejre Municipality on Zealand, Denmark, with Iron Age and Viking Age occupation - the site the text is about [SUPPORTED: https:/